                <pb facs="0979" n="867" sameAs="frus:frus1935v03/pg867"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.001H61/77: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Johnson</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peiping</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-08-09T15:00:00+08:00">August 9, 1935—3
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received August 9—9:15 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>408. Legation’s 406. Following from American Consul General at Shanghai.</p>
                <quote rend="blockquote">
                    <p>“August 8, 2 p.m. Referring to my telegram of August 7, 4 p.m. Japanese Vice
                        Consul called today with the suggestion that I should write to the Chairman
                        of the Municipal Council and request the police to suppress the copies of
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Vanity Fair</hi> that have been delivered and to
                        prevent the sale of other copies. I informed Terasaki that I did not
                        consider that this could be done and at the same time protect American
                        interests.”</p>
                </quote>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
